const faq = [
  { q: 'What materials does MJF print?', a: "HP's nylon range for MJF is the High Reusability family: PA 12 as the standard, PA 11 for ductility, and PA 12 Glass Beads for stiffness and dimensional stability. PA 12 has a full page in the material database with HP datasheet values; the PA 11 and glass-beads datasheets are being re-verified before their values are published here." },
  { q: 'Is MJF faster than SLS?', a: 'Per layer, yes in most cases: MJF fuses a whole layer in a fixed number of passes regardless of how many parts it contains, while SLS laser time scales with cross-section area. Whole-job turnaround also depends on cooling and post-processing, which both processes share.' },
  { q: 'What colour are MJF parts?', a: 'Raw MJF parts come out grey because of the fusing agent; they are very commonly dyed black for a uniform finish. SLS parts, by contrast, are white or off-white and accept dye in a wide range of colours.' },
  { q: 'Does MJF need supports?', a: 'No. Like SLS, the powder bed supports the parts, so MJF prints complex geometries and nested builds without support structures.' },
];
