  <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/processes/">Processes</a> / MJF</p>
  <h1>MJF: Multi Jet Fusion</h1>
  <div class="quick-answer">
    <p class="qa-label">Quick answer</p>
    <p>MJF is HP's powder-bed fusion process: inkjet arrays print fusing and detailing agents onto nylon powder, and an infrared lamp fuses the printed areas. It produces PA12, PA11 and glass-bead-filled parts with fine detail, consistent properties and fast layer times — the main alternative to SLS for production nylon parts.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>How MJF works</h2>
  <p>Each layer starts as a fresh bed of nylon powder. A carriage carrying inkjet heads sweeps the bed and deposits two liquids: a fusing agent wherever the part's cross-section should melt, and a detailing agent around its edges to keep boundaries sharp. A second carriage exposes the bed to infrared energy; only the agent-treated powder absorbs enough energy to fuse. Because fusing happens across the whole layer at once, layer time does not depend on how much of the layer is part — a key difference from laser-based SLS.</p>

  <h2>Materials</h2>
  <p>HP's nylon range is built around reusability: unfused powder is recovered and used again at a manufacturer-specified refresh ratio. The flagship grade is <a href="/materials/hp-pa-12/">HP PA 12</a>, the balanced standard; the range also includes a PA 11 grade where ductility and impact resistance matter, and a glass-beads grade for stiffer, more dimensionally stable parts. HP's current datasheets for those two grades could not be re-verified at this site's publication date, so — per our <a href="/editorial/">sourcing policy</a> — only PA 12 carries a data page for now; the others will be added when their datasheets are verified.</p>

  <h2>Strengths and limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Strengths:</strong> fast, content-independent layer times; fine features and sharp edges from the detailing agent; consistent, near-isotropic properties; a streamlined powder-recovery workflow, especially with HP's processing stations.</li>
    <li><strong>Limits:</strong> parts emerge grey and are usually dyed black, so colour work is less flexible than SLS; the material catalogue is narrower than SLS (one machine maker, a focused material set); hollow parts still need escape holes, as covered in the <a href="/guides/designing-for-sls-mjf/">design guide</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>SLS or MJF?</h2>
  <p>The two processes overlap heavily in applications. The detailed, sourced comparison — mechanics, materials, colour, economics — is at <a href="/compare/sls-vs-mjf/">SLS vs MJF</a>.</p>

  <Faq items={faq} />
